/*
 * Main SRAM is split in two so the process memory pool has a fixed
 * home of its own.
 *
 * progbase used to be an ordinary BSS array: the linker placed it
 * after whatever the kernel happened to contain, so every build that
 * changed the kernel's size silently moved every process image.  That
 * corrupted user memory in ways that looked like application bugs.
 *
 * RAM holds the kernel and nothing else; PROGPOOL is exactly USERMEM
 * and runs to the top of main SRAM, where the scratch banks begin.
 * If the kernel outgrows its half the link now fails with "region RAM
 * overflowed" instead of quietly relocating user memory.
 *
 * Main SRAM is 512K and this split uses ALL of it: RAM + PROGPOOL is
 * exactly 0x80000.  There is no slack anywhere, so the two numbers
 * below only ever move against each other.
 *
 * The kernel is no longer PICO_COPY_TO_RAM.  Most of it executes from
 * flash (linker_overrides/default_text_excludes.incl names what), which
 * took kernel code in RAM from 90,676 bytes to 70,472 and left the
 * region 47K larger than it needs to be.  That is where the pool grew
 * from: 312K originally, 308K when the text primitives needed 4K, and
 * 340K now.
 *
 * The kernel currently uses 160,520 bytes of the 176,128 below, so
 * there is about 15K of headroom.  If it runs out the link fails with
 * "region RAM overflowed", which is the point - it is a loud error, not
 * a silent relocation of user memory.  The valve when that happens is
 * to move more code to flash rather than to take memory back from the
 * pool.
 *
 * A process ceiling is not PROGSIZE (which is the whole pool) but
 * "leave room for the biggest neighbour to be resident" -
 * largest_neighbour() in swapper.c.
 *
 * KEEP THIS IN STEP WITH TOTALMEM in the Makefile: config.h sizes
 * progbase[] from USERMEM and the two must describe the same region.
 * Nothing checks that they agree.
 */
MEMORY
{
    RAM(rwx)     : ORIGIN = RAM_ORIGIN,           LENGTH = 0x2C000
    PROGPOOL(rw) : ORIGIN = RAM_ORIGIN + 0x2C000, LENGTH = 336k
}
